# Build a three-walled room, carve a doorway through the far wall, apply the
# cut, then play the level and walk out through the hole.
#
# Coordinates:
#   w(x,y,z)  a point in the level, projected by the editor's own camera
#   up(h)     extrude the brush being drawn to h units tall
#   cx()/cy() pixel offsets from the window's client origin, for dock buttons
#             and the viewport toolbar -- neither lives inside the 3D viewport,
#             so a viewport fraction cannot address them
#
# HammerForge draws in two stages: drag the base footprint across the grid
# plane, then move up and click to set the height. Every drag/click pair below
# is one brush.
#
# Two constraints shape the layout, and both come from the draw tool rather
# than from taste:
#
# 1. The tool raycasts against existing brush faces before falling back to the
#    grid plane, so ground hidden behind a wall cannot be clicked -- the click
#    lands on the wall. Wall footprints are therefore disjoint, and each is
#    drawn before anything that would shadow it.
# 2. There is no floor brush. Cuts extrude upward from the grid plane, so a
#    doorway cut through a wall also cuts any floor brush beneath it and leaves
#    a pit at the threshold. The walls stand on the ground plane that New Level
#    provides, which puts the doorway flush with it.
#
# The near side is left open so the camera can see into the room.

# Open on the empty grid so the first action reads clearly.
move w(0,0,0) 500
dwell 1300

# --- Far wall, the one the doorway goes through --------------------------
move w(-48,0,-48) 800
dwell 450
press
move w(48,0,-32) 1300
dwell 500
release
dwell 700
move up(48) 700
dwell 700
click
dwell 1200

# --- Left wall ------------------------------------------------------------
# Starts at the far wall's near face rather than its far corner: the far
# corner's ground is now inside the wall's shadow.
move w(-48,0,-32) 700
dwell 350
press
move w(-32,0,48) 1000
dwell 500
release
dwell 700
move up(48) 700
dwell 700
click
dwell 1000

# --- Right wall -----------------------------------------------------------
move w(32,0,-32) 700
dwell 350
press
move w(48,0,48) 1000
dwell 500
release
dwell 700
move up(48) 700
dwell 700
click
dwell 1500

# --- Switch to Cutout -----------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately slow: this is the point of the clip, and the mode change needs
# to register before the geometry changes.
move cx(295) cy(317) 1100
dwell 800
click
dwell 1100

# --- Carve the doorway ----------------------------------------------------
# The cut brush runs from inside the room out past the back of the wall. It has
# to overshoot: the ground immediately behind the wall is in the wall's shadow
# and cannot be clicked, so the far end is placed beyond where the shadow ends.
# Overshooting costs nothing -- the parts outside the wall have nothing to
# subtract from -- and it guarantees the cut goes cleanly through rather than
# leaving a skin. It stops at 32 of the wall's 48 units so what is left reads
# as a doorway rather than a missing section.
move w(-16,0,-16) 900
dwell 400
press
move w(16,0,-128) 1200
dwell 500
release
dwell 700
move up(32) 700
dwell 700
click
dwell 1700

# --- Apply the cut --------------------------------------------------------
# Until this lands the cut is only staged: the toolbar counts it as pending and
# the wall is still whole.
move cx(1147) cy(141) 1000
dwell 800
click
dwell 1600

# --- Test the level -------------------------------------------------------
# HammerForge does not run full CSG live -- brushes stay lightweight preview
# nodes and the boolean only resolves at bake. Test Level bakes, hides the
# drafts and drops a first-person player in, which is the only path that shows
# the doorway as a hole rather than as a staged cut.
move cx(267) cy(239) 900
dwell 600
click
dwell 900
move cx(125) cy(425) 800
dwell 700
click

# --- Cut to the playtest window -------------------------------------------
# The playtest opens as its own window, "hammerforge (DEBUG)", in its own
# process, and the editor's window stops presenting frames the moment it does
# -- a capture left pointing at the editor goes black, so the cut is not
# optional.
#
# The timing is tight on both sides. The window does not exist for the first
# few seconds after the click, and about twelve seconds in the editor hands the
# game's window over to the game's own process, which destroys the one being
# captured. Everything worth showing has to happen in between.
dwell 4200
obs capture --scene HammerForgeGame (debug) 4.7
dwell 500

# --- Walk out through the doorway -----------------------------------------
# The spawn sits at the centre of the room facing -Z, which is the wall the
# doorway was cut through. Sprint: at HammerForge's default grid the player is
# small next to the room, and a walk would not clear the wall before the
# handover above takes the window away.
keydown shift
key W 2500
keyup shift
dwell 1600
